---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ProjectCard from '../components/ProjectCard.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Research"
  description="Research by John Rick Manzanares in topological data analysis, bone microstructure, computational morphology, scientific computing, and machine learning."
>
  <header class="page-hero shell">
    <p class="kicker">Research</p>
    <h1>From shape to measurable structure.</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      My work sits between mathematics, computation, and scientific data. I am particularly interested
      in descriptors that retain meaningful structure while remaining interpretable enough to connect
      mathematical features with physical questions.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section" id="bone-fragility">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Current doctoral research</p>
          <h2>Topology and bone fragility</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Trabecular bone is a three-dimensional network whose mechanical behaviour depends on more
          than local thickness or volume fraction. I study whether topological summaries can complement
          conventional morphometry when describing this architecture.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="actions research-interface-link">
        <a class="button" href="/research/topology-bone-fragility/">Open the interactive research interface</a>
      </div>

      <div class="stack">
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Persistent homology of micro-CT images</h3>
          <p>
            Cubical persistent homology is used to track connected components, tunnels, and cavities
            across filtrations derived from image intensity and signed-distance information. Vector
            representations such as persistence images make these summaries usable in statistical models.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Structure–strength modelling</h3>
          <p>
            Topological descriptors are compared with standard bone morphometry in small-sample
            regression settings. The emphasis is on careful validation, interpretable feature families,
            and avoiding claims that exceed what the cohort can support.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Directional and frame-invariant descriptors</h3>
          <p>
            A related line of work develops local and directional descriptors for anisotropic bone
            architecture, including orientation fields and topological measurements designed to remain
            meaningful under changes of coordinate frame.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="morphometry">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Scientific computing</p>
          <h2>Computational morphology at scale</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Large volumetric images make seemingly simple descriptors expensive. I work on implementations
          that make morphology and topology practical on high-resolution three-dimensional data.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="project-grid">
        <ProjectCard
          eyebrow="Open research software"
          title="bone_morphometry"
          description="A Rust implementation focused on transparent, reviewable computation of bone descriptors and reproducible research workflows."
          href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/bone_morphometry"
          tags={['Rust', 'morphometry', 'reproducibility']}
          kind="morphology"
          external
        />
        <ProjectCard
          eyebrow="Large-volume topology"
          title="Streaming topological summaries"
          description="Memory-conscious computation of Betti and Minkowski-type summaries for volumes that are too large to treat as a single dense in-memory object."
          href="/software/"
          tags={['streaming', 'Betti numbers', 'large images']}
          kind="bone"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="ball-mapper">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Topological data analysis</p>
          <h2>Ball Mapper and range queries</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Ball Mapper is attractive because it gives a compact graph-based view of data. My work examines
          how its cover construction can be accelerated while keeping the mathematical meaning of the
          algorithm explicit.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Exact and approximate neighbourhood search</h3>
          <p>
            The implementation separates the Ball Mapper construction from the range-query oracle,
            allowing exact and approximate search backends to be studied systematically with attention
            to boundary conventions, invariance, and empirical performance.
          </p>
          <div class="inline-links">
            <a href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/fast-ballmapper">fast-ballmapper →</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/jhnrckmnznrs/probabilistic_ball_mapper">probabilistic_ball_mapper →</a>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="scientific-ml">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Exploratory direction</p>
          <h2>Topology-aware scientific machine learning</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          I am interested in models that learn mappings between fields while respecting scientifically
          meaningful structure, including neural operators and physics-informed representations.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Neural operators</h3>
          <p>
            A central question is how topological summaries or losses can complement operator-learning
            architectures when the desired output is a field rather than a single scalar prediction.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Earlier applied machine learning</h3>
          <p>
            Before the doctoral project, I worked on computer-vision methods for plastic pollution
            monitoring and on topology-informed classification of electrocardiogram data. These projects
            continue to shape my preference for methods that connect computation with a concrete scientific question.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
